## 3. Codebase Analysis & Strategic Guidance

The following analysis is based on my direct review of the current codebase. Use these notes and tips to guide your implementation.

### Relevant Existing Code
*   **File:** `modules/core-platform/src/main/java/villagecompute/storefront/media/VideoJobHandler.java`
    *   **Summary:** Application-scoped job handler that injects `MediaWorkerConfig`, `MediaProcessor`, `MediaStorageClient`, and repositories to coordinate video processing. It enforces feature flag kill switches, uses a semaphore sized by `ffmpeg.maxConcurrent`, wraps work in a Micrometer `Timer`, extracts metadata via `mediaProcessor`, uploads HLS playlists/segments + MP4/poster derivatives to R2, and persists `MediaDerivative` rows with tenant context, while incrementing success/failure/timeout counters.
    *   **Recommendation:** When extending behavior (HLS variants, retries, new metrics), reuse the existing helper methods (`processVideoInternal`, `uploadVideoDerivatives`) and keep semaphore/timeouts aligned with config. Any new derivative types must still call `MediaStoragePathBuilder` and persist via `MediaDerivativeRepository` to keep metadata + quotas accurate.
*   **File:** `modules/core-platform/src/main/java/villagecompute/storefront/media/MediaWorkerConfig.java`
    *   **Summary:** SmallRye `@ConfigMapping` for the `media.processing` namespace exposing nested groups (`worker`, `ffmpeg`, `video`, `thumbnailator`, `image`). Defaults capture dispatch intervals, semaphore size, path to FFmpeg binary, timeout values, and poster/HLS toggles.
    *   **Recommendation:** Inject this config wherever FFmpeg parameters are needed (handlers, processors, Kubernetes env) rather than hardcoding numbers. If you introduce new tuning knobs (e.g., max segment bitrate), add them here so they flow through Quarkus config, env vars, and deployment manifests consistently.
*   **File:** `modules/core-platform/src/main/java/villagecompute/storefront/media/MediaProcessor.java`
    *   **Summary:** Defines the abstraction for image/video work with nested DTOs (`VideoProcessingResult`, `HLSVariant`, `VideoMetadata`, etc.) that implementations such as the FFmpeg-backed processor return. Handlers call `processVideo` to obtain playlists, segments, poster frames, and optional MP4 files, and `extractVideoMetadata` to populate the `MediaAsset` record.
    *   **Recommendation:** Stick to this interface for all derivative needs—update implementations (e.g., FFmpeg command builder) instead of branching inside the handler. Reuse the provided metadata objects when persisting width/height/duration so downstream services remain schema-compatible.
*   **File:** `modules/core-platform/src/test/java/villagecompute/storefront/media/VideoJobHandlerTest.java`
    *   **Summary:** Quarkus test suite using stubbed media processor/storage beans and real repositories to cover the handler. Tests assert semaphore enforcement by running concurrent jobs, validate metrics counters/timers, ensure HLS playlists/segments + poster/MP4 derivatives are persisted, and verify metadata extraction + failure handling.
    *   **Recommendation:** Use this suite as the safety net for new scenarios (timeouts, retries, kill switches). Keep helper methods (`createTestAsset*`, `createFakeVideoFile`) and tenant setup intact so additional assertions remain deterministic without relying on actual FFmpeg binaries.

### Implementation Tips & Notes
*   **Tip:** The Kubernetes deployment in `infra/kustomize/base/media-worker-deployment.yaml` already sets env vars for every `media.processing.*` property, pins high CPU/memory limits, mounts `/tmp` storage, and limits work to CRITICAL queue items—carry any new config keys through this manifest (and overlays) so operators can tune FFmpeg without redeploying code.
*   **Tip:** Local developers rely on `docker/docker-compose.yml` (PostgreSQL + MinIO + mocks); document any additional prerequisites (FFmpeg binaries, separate worker profile) in the README section referenced by this task so the “docs describe scaling + troubleshooting” criterion is satisfied.
*   **Tip:** Metrics emitted here (`media.video.transcode.*`) feed the operational dashboards described in §3.12, so avoid renaming them; if you add gauges (e.g., active FFmpeg processes), register them with Micrometer and expose via `/q/metrics` to match Prometheus scraping annotations already present on the worker deployment.
*   **Tip:** Queue payloads arrive via DelayedJob with versioning—if you touch payload shapes or add job-level metadata (e.g., desired rendition set), increment or handle versions defensively to avoid tripping the “Background Job Poison Pill” runbook path.
*   **Warning:** Tenant context is propagated through `@ActivateRequestContext` and repository lookups; never cache the injected `TenantContext` globally, and ensure any spawned threads/process callbacks clean up temporary directories plus release the semaphore even on exceptions to prevent deadlocks.
